<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Finding a point in the third quadrant</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>
        A point <m>P</m> on the unit circle satisfies
        \(\sin\theta = -\dfrac{@sin_num@}{@hyp@}\)
        and lies in the third quadrant.
    </p>

    <ol marker="(a)">
        <li>
            <p>Find \(\cos\theta\).</p>
            <p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>Find \(\tan\theta\).</p>
            <p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>Write down the exact coordinates of \(P\\cos\theta,\sin\theta)\).</p>
            <p>
                P=[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                What is the reference angle for \(\theta\)?
                Leave your answer as an exact expression.
            </p>
            <p>[[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>
        </li>
    </ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>
<p>
    Recall the Pythagorean identity:
    \(\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=1\).
</p>

<p>
    Substituting the given sine value:
</p>

<p>
    \[
    \cos^2\theta
    =1-\left(-\frac{@sin_num@}{@hyp@}\right)^2
    \]
</p>

<p>
    \[
    =\frac{@hyp^2-sin_num^2@}{@hyp^2@}
    =\frac{@cos_num^2@}{@hyp^2@}.
    \]
</p>

<p>
    Therefore, \(\cos\theta=\pm\frac{@cos_num@}{@hyp@}\).
    Since \(\theta\) lies in the third quadrant, cosine is negative.
</p>

<p>
    Hence,
    \[
    \cos\theta=-\frac{@cos_num@}{@hyp@}.
    \]
</p>

<p><strong>(b) Finding \(\tan\theta\)</strong></p>

<p>
    Using \(\tan\theta=\frac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta}\):
</p>

<p>
    \[
    \tan\theta=
    \frac{-\frac{@sin_num@}{@hyp@}}
    {-\frac{@cos_num@}{@hyp@}}
    =\frac{@sin_num@}{@cos_num@}.
    \]
</p>

<p>
    Therefore, \(\tan\theta=\frac{@sin_num@}{@cos_num@}\).
    Tangent is positive in the third quadrant.
</p>

<p><strong>(c) Finding the coordinates of P</strong></p>

<p>
    A point on the unit circle has coordinates
    \(P=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)\).
</p>

<p>
    Therefore,
    \[
    P=\left(
    -\frac{@cos_num@}{@hyp@},
    -\frac{@sin_num@}{@hyp@}
    \right).
    \]
</p>

<p><strong>(d) Finding the reference angle</strong></p>

<p>
    The reference angle is the acute angle between the terminal arm
    and the negative \(x\)-axis.
</p>

<p>
    In the third quadrant, its tangent is positive. Thus,
</p>

<p>
    \[
    \tan\alpha=
    \frac{|\sin\theta|}{|\cos\theta|}
    =\frac{@sin_num@}{@cos_num@}.
    \]
</p>

<p>
    Hence, the exact reference angle is
    \[
    \alpha=\arctan\left(\frac{@sin_num@}{@cos_num@}\right).
    \]
</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
